PROCESS - Computah OS library
(generated from docs/API.md)

The scheduler. Every program runs as a thread (a
coroutine); the scheduler waits for signals on
behalf of all threads and gives every waiting
thread every signal. A process is a thread
together with its data: environment variables,
working directory and standard streams, inherited
from the thread that started it. Most programs use
thread, event and shell instead.

process.spawn(fn[, name[, options]]) -> thread

Starts fn in a new thread, a child of the running
one. Options: detached (keep running after the
parent ends), args (packed arguments for fn), data
(process data to use instead of inheriting).
Returns the thread record (methods below).

process.current() -> thread

The running thread, or nil inside event listeners
and timers.

process.byId(id) -> thread

A thread by id, or nil.

process.list() -> table

All threads: {id, name, status, parent, detached,
uptime} each.

process.info([thread]) -> table

OpenOS-style information about a process (default:
the running one; also accepts an id): {id, path,
command, env, data, status, parent}. path is the
program file, command its name. Returns nil,
reason if there is none.

process.running() -> path, env, command

The running process's program path, environment
and name.

process.data() -> table

The running process's data: {env, cwd, io =
{stdin, stdout, stderr}, path}.

process.load(program[, env[, init[, name]]]) -> coroutine

Loads a program file (or wraps a function) as a
coroutine, like OpenOS. Resume it to run it with
arguments. env is the program's environment
(default: a new one based on the globals); init is
called inside the coroutine first. Returns nil,
reason if the file can't be loaded. Waiting inside
it works normally.

  local co = process.load("/home/job.lua")
  coroutine.resume(co, "arg1")

process.interrupt(thread)

Makes the thread's current or next wait raise
interrupted (what Ctrl+C does to the foreground
program).

process.run()

Runs the scheduler forever. Only the kernel calls
this.

Thread methods

The records returned by process.spawn (and wrapped
by thread.create):

Method - Description
t:getStatus() - "running" (the caller), "ready",
    "waiting", "suspended" or "dead".
t:join([timeout]) - Waits until it ends; true, or
    false on timeout.
t:kill() - Ends it (and its attached children).
t:suspend() / t:resume() - Pause it (it gets no
    signals) / let it continue.
t:detach() / t:attach([parent]) - Survive / don't
    survive the parent ending.
t:results() - What its function returned, or nil,
    error.
t.id, t.name, t.data, t.error - Fields.
